#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184052 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184142 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3155a85f-7bd8-584c-a5ae-e2e18dd60cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/id #uuid "c049aaef-f59a-5113-b739-20cc7a6d802c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3520" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin u = \\dfrac{3}{4}, \\qquad \\cos u =  -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(u + v\\right) = \\dfrac{a- b\\sqrt 7}{20},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u+v) = \\sin u \\cos v+ \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u},$ $\\cos{u},$ and $\\cos{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\sin{v},$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 v + \\cos^2 v= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 v = 1- \\cos^2 v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{4}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25-16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\sin v= \\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{4}{5})+(-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{20}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-3\\sqrt{7}}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=12, b=3,$ and $a+b = 15.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184132 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99d7256f-6130-52b4-8188-3b1388c63f2b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, or Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/id #uuid "06df772e-eb43-55e8-9ede-3fc2f7ae291d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9984" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u = \\dfrac{7}{25}, \\qquad \\sin v = \\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac {3} {5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, find the value of $\\sin(u-v).$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u \\cos v- \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\cos{u}$, $\\sin{v}$, and $\\cos{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\sin{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 u = 1- \\cos^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡u & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{7}{25})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{7}^{2}}{{25}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{625}-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{576}{625}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{576}{625}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{24}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\sin u= -\\dfrac{24}{25}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sin(u - v) \n\t\t\t&= \\sin u \\cos v - \\cos u \\sin v \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\left( -\\dfrac {24}{25} \\right)\\left(\\dfrac {3} {5}\\right)\n \t\t\t\t -  \\left(\\dfrac {7}{25}\\right) \\left( \\dfrac {4} {5}\\right) \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\n \t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {72} {125} -  \\dfrac {28} {125} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {100} {125}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {4} {5}.\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184142 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3155a85f-7bd8-584c-a5ae-e2e18dd60cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13d91780-96a0-5b05-9282-837d0bc5cf2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550411} {:answer/id #uuid "23fabac7-34be-5901-889d-cbd6475a113f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550454} {:answer/id #uuid "87f86819-67b7-5681-bbb3-b9db74260b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550711} {:answer/id #uuid "9975a3bb-76fc-5c77-ae49-5724d89ccc20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550757} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b4e39f-b8d2-5d43-9273-97be2dbccbc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550906}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23fabac7-34be-5901-889d-cbd6475a113f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550454} :answer-field/id #uuid "826a30ca-938a-5809-9560-1e4fb2a1a3e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97834400-1835-5bc3-9180-5bacac383a8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-119317" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a-b\\sqrt{1}4}{6},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6}-\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-1\\sqrt{14}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=2,b=1,$ and $a+b=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551344 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184142 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3155a85f-7bd8-584c-a5ae-e2e18dd60cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f0f4507-f336-56f6-86cc-8b6c67aa2f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550435} {:answer/id #uuid "77a7b2c9-147e-5fc7-9ea6-9c47b3f3c127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550669} {:answer/id #uuid "cf06079f-488c-54c6-9f67-6f54375dcb2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550926} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5c049f-d4ae-506d-8359-38c91b104d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550968} {:answer/id #uuid "f4e4f716-0f36-5041-8ce8-3e1332c0fd72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf06079f-488c-54c6-9f67-6f54375dcb2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550926} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b2f076d-ce9b-57f9-98c4-9fb84d87fd00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dbced5cb-cb8f-554f-bc5c-eb4739e6f20e" :question/math-academy-id "q-119320" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{2}{7},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v).$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})+(\\frac{2}{3})(-\\frac{2}{7}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{21}-\\frac{4}{21} \\\\  & =-\\frac{19}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184142 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3155a85f-7bd8-584c-a5ae-e2e18dd60cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "236be099-00d2-5a93-807c-c0611363dd3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250857} {:answer/id #uuid "936b5a6a-37a9-599d-94c5-7ae7a2280c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251816} {:answer/id #uuid "b6bc72ad-8c01-5974-9602-15da4003854b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252163} {:answer/id #uuid "c9b770b1-d1d3-52d1-84af-ef96a46675ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252340} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8f375c-b6b5-5356-9e90-212803b05a94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "936b5a6a-37a9-599d-94c5-7ae7a2280c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251816} :answer-field/id #uuid "42b3f8d2-ad82-503b-8cd5-80330391b6f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1267137-e6ff-5440-b525-7d34538d465f" :question/math-academy-id "q-119451" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{2}{7},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a-b\\sqrt{55}}{42},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{11})^{2}}{{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{11}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{36}-\\frac{11}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{36-11}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6})(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})+(\\frac{5}{6})(\\frac{2}{7}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{55}}{42}+\\frac{10}{42} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-3\\sqrt{55}}{42}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=10,b=3,$ and $a+b=13.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184142 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3155a85f-7bd8-584c-a5ae-e2e18dd60cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d409728-f16b-5dbd-989e-4297f678c27d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550481} {:answer/id #uuid "4c87b11b-03eb-50d1-a061-8f5cba3a6ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550548} {:answer/id #uuid "505afb7e-87e3-5258-8fa9-547c7b39ab3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550557} {:answer/id #uuid "958cbb92-f043-53d1-bb9b-025a90326fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550744} {:answer/id #uuid "bde96f49-8d44-5f49-af06-0fedc038fdc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "958cbb92-f043-53d1-bb9b-025a90326fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550744} :answer-field/id #uuid "d88de4d6-11e4-59ab-b35f-5b9cbf6e3f93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7374391-644b-5fe8-92d1-774c6f2685b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-141359" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\frac{\\sqrt{a}-\\sqrt{b}}{4},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=6,b=2,$ and $a+b=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99d7256f-6130-52b4-8188-3b1388c63f2b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, or Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "431f436d-286f-5124-9f57-838cbb663593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550525} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d170e4-09b8-54a6-95dc-732e3918ff88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550908} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d301bb-3dab-58d3-b3c7-a4b5e4f95d3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550936} {:answer/id #uuid "f71b7119-0fb5-5212-83f2-49afd258fc8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551032} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6b2054-d008-528a-bf1c-110725aeb7d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f71b7119-0fb5-5212-83f2-49afd258fc8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551032} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b2abad3-93fb-5e4a-a512-cc96b4e71bd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4643f6f8-6def-57fc-9f05-9106c1781f57" :question/math-academy-id "q-141389" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{3}-b\\sqrt{7}}{8},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{8}-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}-1\\sqrt{7}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=3,b=1,$ and $a+b=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99d7256f-6130-52b4-8188-3b1388c63f2b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, or Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8063e78c-b15b-5158-9362-eae8ddeee9b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{5}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550693} {:answer/id #uuid "86c77418-8966-566d-a663-c864416cbf71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+\\sqrt{15}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550708} {:answer/id #uuid "dc24debd-ea85-5a42-99f4-725ec24bcaa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-\\sqrt{15}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550966} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8359f2-1d22-508a-92f8-0e7f72b7d4ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{5}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551043} {:answer/id #uuid "fdcce495-5552-55ea-89df-1f5a9fdec70c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc24debd-ea85-5a42-99f4-725ec24bcaa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-\\sqrt{15}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550966} :answer-field/id #uuid "45d5c494-ab4a-516a-a05d-cf2961b1ecb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8787df3-5e2e-5b10-9759-f8010831da06" :question/math-academy-id "q-144400" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v).$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡u=1-{cos}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡u & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{4}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{4}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})-(-\\frac{2}{3})(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{6}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-\\sqrt{15}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184142 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3155a85f-7bd8-584c-a5ae-e2e18dd60cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4a6ccf5-14ce-5806-ace1-7ca838a6fd7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{55}-10}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4a6ccf5-14ce-5806-ace1-7ca838a6fd7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{55}-10}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550943} :answer-field/id #uuid "82fa694a-25f2-5e7d-ab30-eca894f443f3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24a538ad-232b-5718-acdd-ce07c94cc6d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-274506" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$\n\nIf $v$ lies in the first quadrant, then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{25}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{36}-\\frac{25}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6})+(-\\frac{2}{3})(\\frac{5}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{55}}{18}-\\frac{10}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{55}-10}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551255 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99d7256f-6130-52b4-8188-3b1388c63f2b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, or Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9ff4504-ac7a-57d3-a336-b4500ec61972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{65}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9ff4504-ac7a-57d3-a336-b4500ec61972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{65}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550820} :answer-field/id #uuid "598d1261-b549-515b-a77a-1a37d5829749" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e61a3ba-7f7b-576c-9651-31afc3ef6f71" :question/math-academy-id "q-326306" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{5}{13},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{12}{13},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nIf $v$ lies in the second quadrant, then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, we use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{13})(-\\frac{3}{5})+(\\frac{12}{13})(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{65}+\\frac{48}{65} \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{65}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99d7256f-6130-52b4-8188-3b1388c63f2b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, or Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df83a4e1-66ba-5873-97da-2d74aacdb5f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df83a4e1-66ba-5873-97da-2d74aacdb5f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550977} :answer-field/id #uuid "24922baa-2702-5f4a-9cd9-809ee303d03c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551085} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fa37d87-2d10-5b67-a6d0-78d0e0a5a54d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fa37d87-2d10-5b67-a6d0-78d0e0a5a54d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550513} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab314b19-c35e-5b2b-9cf9-5dbd3ed37713" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bb1f9724-9227-5181-ae8d-25a299447d4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-326377" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{8}{17},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{15}{17},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}}$\\sqrt{3}\\,-\\,$ {{field-2}} | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $34$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{17})(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+(\\frac{15}{17})(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{34}-\\frac{15}{34} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}-15}{34}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99d7256f-6130-52b4-8188-3b1388c63f2b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, or Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a040c0f-a1fd-58ed-aad2-fe5b5cf047d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a040c0f-a1fd-58ed-aad2-fe5b5cf047d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550471} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7103d3e-80a2-5de4-ac0e-a10a2142cb05" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551165} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1710ed1-4caf-58bf-82be-5d414220e087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1710ed1-4caf-58bf-82be-5d414220e087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550984} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed949f4a-0931-51ac-8da5-094e551428fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9ecd9133-5002-52de-bbff-55ef2618e8b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-326383" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{5}{13},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{12}{13},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}}$\\,\\,+\\,$ {{field-2}}$\\sqrt{7}$ | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $52$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{16}-\\frac{9}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{13})(\\frac{3}{4})-(\\frac{12}{13})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{52}+\\frac{12\\sqrt{7}}{52} \\\\  & =\\frac{15+12\\sqrt{7}}{52}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
